3 Error handling
****************

The ‘--on-error’ option selects what bocl does when an _unhandled_
‘SERIOUS-CONDITION’ reaches the top level -- an error your own code did
not catch with ‘HANDLER-CASE’, ‘IGNORE-ERRORS’, or a restart.  The
action is published to Lisp as ‘BOCL:*ON-ERROR*’.  When ‘--on-error’ is
_not_ given, option processing, the init/rc files and ‘-l’ / ‘-x’ forms
run under ‘exit’ (fail hard, good for batch), while the interactive REPL
instead defaults to ‘debug’.

   An ‘--on-error’ given _before_ the first ‘-i’ (or ‘--no-init’) sets
the policy used to load the rc and init files; one given _after_ the
first ‘-i’ is processed in command-line order together with the ‘-l’ /
‘-x’ forms -- it behaves exactly like ‘-x '(setf bocl:*on-error*
ACTION)'’ and applies to everything that follows it until the next
‘--on-error’.  If any ‘--on-error’ is given, the REPL uses the last
value in effect; otherwise it defaults to ‘debug’.

‘exit’
     _At the REPL:_ report the condition and exit the process (status
     1).  _While loading a file:_ the error aborts the load and the
     process exits.  This is the fail-hard mode for batch use, scripts,
     and CI.
‘debug’
     Enter the interactive debugger (‘INVOKE-DEBUGGER’): condition
     banner, backtrace, restart menu, and a debug REPL. If the debugger
     is continued, control returns to the prompt.  The natural choice
     for interactive development.  *Note Debugger::.
‘abort’
     Report the condition and return to the top-level REPL -- no
     debugger, the process stays alive.  While loading a file, the
     failing form aborts the whole load.
‘appease’
     The same as ‘abort’ at the REPL. While loading a file, however, a
     failing top-level form is reported (‘;; [load] skipped a form:
     ...’) and skipped, and loading continues to the end (skips are
     counted in ‘*LOAD-ERROR-COUNT*’).  This is meant for loading large
     or partially-supported suites (e.g. the SACLA / ANSI tests) so they
     reach their final report instead of dying on the first unsupported
     form.

   So: ‘exit’ fails hard; ‘debug’ opens the debugger; ‘abort’ returns to
the REPL; and ‘appease’ differs from ‘abort’ only for ‘LOAD’, where it
skips bad forms and keeps going.  When no ‘--on-error’ is given the
running default is ‘exit’ for processing and ‘debug’ for the interactive
REPL.


